lessons.body:
# Render vs Commit: The Two Phases

Every React update goes through two phases. Confusing them is how side effects end up in the wrong place.

## Render phase: pure calculation

React calls your components and collects element objects. This phase must be **pure**: no network calls, no subscriptions, no DOM writes, no mutating refs. React may pause it, restart it, or throw the result away entirely (concurrent rendering) — anything with side effects here can run twice or never.

This is why StrictMode double-invokes your component functions in development: it's *proving* your render is pure by running it twice and checking nothing breaks.

## Commit phase: apply and react

Once React has the new element tree, it diffs it against the previous one and writes the minimal DOM updates. Then it runs effects: layout effects synchronously before paint, passive effects after. **Side effects belong here** — this is what `useEffect` is for.

```jsx
function UserCard({ id }) {
  // RENDER: pure — just describe
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);

  // COMMIT-adjacent: side effects live in useEffect, not in the body
  useEffect(() => {
    fetchUser(id).then(setUser);
  }, [id]);

  if (!user) return <Skeleton />;
  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
}
```

**The one-sentence model:** render turns components into element descriptions (pure, repeatable, disposable); commit turns descriptions into DOM changes and runs effects. Depth on *how* the diffing works comes in the reconciliation module — for now, keep renders pure and put side effects in effects.
